First Author: Shaw
Title: Multicenter analysis of immunosuppressive medications on the risk of malignancy following adult solid organ transplantation
Source number: 45  ￼

Very short summary: This multicenter U.S. retrospective cohort study evaluated how maintenance immunosuppressive drugs relate to post-transplant malignancy across adult solid-organ recipients. Five key findings were: (1) 5,591 patients with 6,142 transplanted organs were included; (2) 517 post-transplant malignancies were identified, with skin cancer the most common at 52.8%; (3) heart and lung recipients initially appeared to have higher malignancy incidence, but this was not significant after adjustment for immunosuppressive medications; (4) random forest variable-importance analysis identified age at transplant and total number of immunosuppressive medications as the strongest predictors; and (5) time-dependent multivariable Cox analysis showed higher cancer risk with azathioprine, cyclosporine, and sirolimus, while tacrolimus was associated with lower risk. Tables/figures used directly included Figures 1–3 and the malignancy/immunosuppression summaries in the main text.  ￼

