	•	Section 3 Comments/Quotes: The primary study aim was epidemiologic risk assessment using time-dependent Cox models, with ML used as a complementary variable-importance analysis, so Primary ML Component is best classified as Other. The authors state that “a random forest model was used to assess variable importance in predicting post-transplant malignancy.” Categorical variables under 10% frequency were grouped, variables were one-hot encoded or normalized, and the model was “fit across a variety of hyperparameters within a Latin hypercube of size 25.” No human comparator or guideline comparator was used. Validation specifics for prediction performance were not reported.  ￼

